You are evaluating information extraction results for PFAS adsorption studies. You will receive:
1. A list of ground-truth records.
2. A list of predicted records.

These records were extracted from the same study through different processes. Your job is to decide whether any predicted record refers to the same meaning as any ground-truth record. Use all fields in the records to match. 

When multiple ground-truth and predicted records differ in a systematic way, such as recurring omission or addition of a prefix or formatting/normalization differences, treat that pattern as likely resulting from extraction or normalization differences rather than true semantic differences. Apply the same matching logic consistently across analogous records.

Each ground-truth record can match at most one predicted record. Each predicted record can match at most one ground-truth record. Match only when you are confident the two records are semantically equivalent. If you are unsure, do not match.

{% if task == "water_type" %}
- Match records if they describe the same water matrix.
- The Class field is useful context, but matching Class alone is not enough.

{% elif task == "adsorbent" %}
- Match records if they refer to the same adsorbent identity in the study.
- Different assignments across the abbreviation, full name, and commercial name fields may still refer to the same record. For example, if one record treats a name as the full name and another treats the same name as the commercial name, they may be considered equivalent as long as both refer to the same adsorbent.
- The category field is useful context, but matching category alone is not enough.
{% elif task == "performance" %}
- Performance_is is constructed as <PFAS_name>|<Adsorbent_id>|<Differentiating_Condition>, they have to match all three slot. 
- PFAS identity differences are material. Do not match different PFAS compounds.
- Adsorbent identity differences are material unless the two adsorbent labels clearly refer to the same adsorbent.
- For the differentiating condition part, do not match if the differentiating condition differs in a material way, such as different water matrix, pH, dose, concentration, contact time, ionic composition, or experimental condition.
{% endif %}

Return strict JSON only, using this schema:

{
  "matches": [
    {
      "gt_index": 0,
      "pred_index": 0,
    }
  ],
  "unmatched_gt_indices": [1, 2],
  "unmatched_pred_indices": [1, 3]
}

Rules for the output:
- Use the provided indices exactly.
- Include only confident semantic matches in "matches".
- Put all unmatched ground-truth indices in "unmatched_gt_indices".
- Put all unmatched prediction indices in "unmatched_pred_indices".
- Do not include any text outside the JSON.

